1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,267
Here are three phase currents,

2
00:00:02,307 --> 00:00:06,124
the kind a motor drive samples thousands
of times a second.

3
00:00:06,164 --> 00:00:09,821
Where is the stator field pointing right
now, and how strong is it?

4
00:00:09,861 --> 00:00:12,915
From three wiggling traces, that's hard to
say.

5
00:00:12,955 --> 00:00:15,916
Combine the three values into one complex
number,

6
00:00:15,956 --> 00:00:19,701
and the answer is an arrow: its angle and
its length.

7
00:00:19,801 --> 00:00:24,466
That arrow is the space vector, and it
opens the control half of this series.

8
00:00:25,766 --> 00:00:28,111
First, the answers from Lesson 10.

9
00:00:28,151 --> 00:00:31,748
One: 8 inductance cycles per mechanical
revolution —

10
00:00:31,788 --> 00:00:36,274
two per electrical cycle, and 4 electrical
cycles per turn.

11
00:00:36,314 --> 00:00:42,516
Two: −10 A on d and 10 A on q give 0.396
N·m in the IPM variant,

12
00:00:42,556 --> 00:00:46,818
about a quarter of it reluctance torque.

13
00:00:46,858 --> 00:00:50,956
Three: an LCR meter reads small-signal,
incremental inductance;

14
00:00:50,996 --> 00:00:56,423
eddy currents, magnet saturation and
coupling to the other phases shift it.

15
00:00:57,723 --> 00:01:00,685
In circuit theory, each sinusoid gets a
phasor:

16
00:01:00,725 --> 00:01:04,725
one fixed complex number holding its
amplitude and phase.

17
00:01:04,765 --> 00:01:07,679
That's perfect for steady state at one
frequency.

18
00:01:07,719 --> 00:01:12,761
But a phasor's angle is a shift in time,
not a direction inside the machine.

19
00:01:12,801 --> 00:01:16,807
A drive needs to know where the stator
field points at every instant

20
00:01:16,847 --> 00:01:20,460
— during start-up, with harmonics, at
changing speed.

21
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:23,275
The geometry for that is already there:

22
00:01:23,315 --> 00:01:27,785
the three winding axes sit 120 degrees
apart, in space.

23
00:01:29,085 --> 00:01:32,343
In Lesson 1, we added the three currents
as vectors,

24
00:01:32,383 --> 00:01:35,216
each along the axis of its own winding.

25
00:01:35,256 --> 00:01:39,218
For balanced currents of amplitude I, the
sum kept a constant length

26
00:01:39,258 --> 00:01:41,954
— three-halves of I — and turned at ω.

27
00:01:41,994 --> 00:01:45,619
Scale that sum by two-thirds, and you have
the space vector.

28
00:01:45,659 --> 00:01:48,685
Its length now equals the phase amplitude.

29
00:01:48,725 --> 00:01:52,788
A bonus: project the vector onto a phase
axis, and you read back

30
00:01:52,828 --> 00:01:57,648
that phase's current — as long as the
three currents add up to zero.

31
00:01:57,688 --> 00:01:59,843
And nothing here assumed sinusoids:

32
00:01:59,883 --> 00:02:03,881
the definition works for any three values,
at any instant.

33
00:02:03,921 --> 00:02:06,763
As a general method for machine
transients,

34
00:02:06,803 --> 00:02:12,798
it's usually credited to Kovács and Rácz,
whose book appeared in Budapest in 1954.

35
00:02:14,098 --> 00:02:18,676
Two-thirds isn't the only choice. Scale by
the square root of two-thirds,

36
00:02:18,716 --> 00:02:21,860
and ten-amp currents give a 12.2-amp
vector.

37
00:02:21,900 --> 00:02:24,868
Why use it? With this power-invariant
scaling,

38
00:02:24,908 --> 00:02:30,106
instantaneous power is simply the real
part of v times the conjugate of i.

39
00:02:30,146 --> 00:02:33,328
With amplitude-invariant scaling, it's
three-halves of that —

40
00:02:33,368 --> 00:02:37,109
the same three-halves that sits in this
series' torque equation.

41
00:02:37,149 --> 00:02:41,517
Check: 10 volts and 10 amps peak, in
phase. The three phases

42
00:02:41,557 --> 00:02:46,719
deliver a steady 150 watts, and both
formulas give exactly that.

43
00:02:46,759 --> 00:02:52,222
This series uses the amplitude-invariant
form. Mix the two up in drive code,

44
00:02:52,262 --> 00:02:56,890
and you're off by 1.5 in power, or 22
percent in current.

45
00:02:58,190 --> 00:03:00,742
So what does the vector point at,
physically?

46
00:03:00,782 --> 00:03:04,669
In Lesson 2, the stator MMF around the gap
was a staircase.

47
00:03:04,709 --> 00:03:08,563
Here's one for a simple two-pole, six-slot
winding.

48
00:03:08,603 --> 00:03:12,611
The steps change shape as the currents
change, but the fundamental,

49
00:03:12,651 --> 00:03:17,099
computed with a Fourier transform, slides
smoothly around the gap.

50
00:03:17,139 --> 00:03:19,954
Its peak sits exactly where the space
vector points,

51
00:03:19,994 --> 00:03:23,418
and its height is proportional to the
vector's length.

52
00:03:23,458 --> 00:03:26,869
The space harmonics of the staircase come
from the winding layout;

53
00:03:26,909 --> 00:03:28,978
the vector doesn't carry them.

54
00:03:30,287 --> 00:03:33,073
Now add the same current to all three
phases.

55
00:03:33,113 --> 00:03:39,511
Three equal arrows, 120 degrees apart, sum
to zero. The space vector doesn't move.

56
00:03:39,551 --> 00:03:41,818
That common part is the zero sequence,

57
00:03:41,858 --> 00:03:46,326
a third of the sum of the phases. We keep
it as a separate number.

58
00:03:46,366 --> 00:03:48,508
In a star connection without neutral,

59
00:03:48,548 --> 00:03:53,166
Kirchhoff's current law at the star point
makes the three currents add to zero:

60
00:03:53,206 --> 00:03:57,676
no zero-sequence current. That's why two
current sensors are enough.

61
00:03:57,716 --> 00:04:00,830
In a delta connection, though, a zero-
sequence current

62
00:04:00,870 --> 00:04:04,640
can circulate inside the delta, unseen at
the terminals.

63
00:04:05,940 --> 00:04:10,026
What if the currents aren't balanced? Say
phase a peaks at only 8 amps,

64
00:04:10,066 --> 00:04:14,362
and the other two at about nine and a half
— still adding to zero.

65
00:04:14,402 --> 00:04:17,242
The tip of the vector now traces an
ellipse.

66
00:04:17,342 --> 00:04:22,502
It splits into two rotating parts: a
forward vector of 9 amps at plus ω,

67
00:04:22,542 --> 00:04:26,035
and a backward vector of 1 amp at minus ω.

68
00:04:26,075 --> 00:04:28,223
Where they line up, they make 10 amps;

69
00:04:28,263 --> 00:04:31,889
where they oppose, 8: the two semi-axes of
the ellipse.

70
00:04:31,929 --> 00:04:35,404
These are the positive- and negative-
sequence components.

71
00:04:35,444 --> 00:04:38,796
If the backward part grows as large as the
forward one,

72
00:04:38,836 --> 00:04:42,913
the ellipse flattens into a line: the
field only pulsates.

73
00:04:44,231 --> 00:04:49,172
Harmonics are just as easy. In a fifth
harmonic, every phase shift is

74
00:04:49,212 --> 00:04:54,481
five times larger: 600 degrees of lag,
which is 120 degrees of lead.

75
00:04:54,521 --> 00:04:56,056
The phase order reverses,

76
00:04:56,096 --> 00:05:00,336
so its space vector turns backward, at
five times the speed.

77
00:05:00,376 --> 00:05:04,672
The seventh turns forward, at 7ω. And
every multiple of three

78
00:05:04,712 --> 00:05:09,722
is identical in all three phases: pure
zero sequence, invisible.

79
00:05:09,762 --> 00:05:12,296
With just five percent of fifth harmonic,

80
00:05:12,336 --> 00:05:16,562
the circle turns into a rounded hexagon:
six bumps per turn.

81
00:05:16,602 --> 00:05:20,837
Seen from a frame turning with the
fundamental, both appear at six times

82
00:05:20,877 --> 00:05:25,355
the frequency: one source of the six-times
ripple common in drives.

83
00:05:26,655 --> 00:05:30,267
Now, data: a synthetic log, so we know the
answer.

84
00:05:30,307 --> 00:05:35,087
Two phase currents: 10 amps at 50 hertz, 5
percent fifth harmonic,

85
00:05:35,127 --> 00:05:38,947
a 200-milliamp offset on channel a, and
noise.

86
00:05:38,987 --> 00:05:41,502
It's in the release folder as a CSV file,

87
00:05:41,542 --> 00:05:45,266
and a few lines of Python turn it into a
space vector.

88
00:05:45,306 --> 00:05:49,642
Phase c comes from the star-point rule;
then it's just the definition.

89
00:05:49,682 --> 00:05:51,541
The locus is a ring at 10 amps:

90
00:05:51,581 --> 00:05:55,350
the fundamental amplitude, read straight
off the plot.

91
00:05:55,390 --> 00:05:59,084
Noise thickens it, the fifth harmonic
makes it a rounded hexagon,

92
00:05:59,124 --> 00:06:03,185
and the offset shifts its centre by about
a quarter of an amp.

93
00:06:04,485 --> 00:06:06,653
Because the space vector is complex,

94
00:06:06,693 --> 00:06:10,672
its Fourier transform tells forward
rotation from backward.

95
00:06:10,712 --> 00:06:15,371
There's a line at plus 50 hertz: 10 amps,
the forward fundamental.

96
00:06:15,411 --> 00:06:19,716
And one at minus 250: half an amp, the
backward fifth.

97
00:06:19,756 --> 00:06:24,176
The line at zero hertz is the offset: 0.23
amps at 30 degrees,

98
00:06:24,216 --> 00:06:28,489
because phase c was computed from the
offset channel.

99
00:06:28,529 --> 00:06:33,819
Next lesson turns fingerprints like these
into a diagnosis of sensor errors.

100
00:06:35,119 --> 00:06:38,205
On a real motor: run it from a drive at
steady load,

101
00:06:38,245 --> 00:06:41,310
and clip current probes onto two phase
leads.

102
00:06:41,350 --> 00:06:44,323
Zero the probes, arrows toward the motor,

103
00:06:44,363 --> 00:06:48,658
and log both channels together, fast
compared with the PWM:

104
00:06:48,698 --> 00:06:54,302
say 100 kilosamples per second for a
20-kilohertz drive. Export a CSV.

105
00:06:54,342 --> 00:06:59,967
Run the same Python. The ring's radius
should match the peak phase current:

106
00:07:00,007 --> 00:07:04,696
a clamp meter reading 7.07 amps RMS means
a 10-amp ring.

107
00:07:04,736 --> 00:07:09,330
PWM ripple shows up as fuzz. If the ring
is squashed or off-centre,

108
00:07:09,370 --> 00:07:11,819
suspect the probes before the motor.

109
00:07:11,859 --> 00:07:16,094
And if the neutral is connected, log all
three phases.

110
00:07:17,394 --> 00:07:19,674
Three predictions before next time.

111
00:07:19,714 --> 00:07:24,968
One: balanced currents of 10 amps peak.
What's the magnitude of the space vector?

112
00:07:26,268 --> 00:07:31,655
Two: add 1 amp of DC to all three phases.
Does the space vector change?

113
00:07:32,955 --> 00:07:38,052
Three: phase c is open, so the phase-a
current is minus the phase-b current.

114
00:07:38,092 --> 00:07:41,160
What does the tip of the vector trace?

115
00:07:42,460 --> 00:07:46,987
Next time, we split this complex number
into two real axes, α and β

116
00:07:47,027 --> 00:07:52,094
— the Clarke transformation — and use it
to diagnose sensor errors.
